# Raw quality failure: source feasibility and disposition review

Prepared 2026-10-26 UTC. This is a CPU/source review. It starts no model, device, communicator or server, changes no runtime, frozen experiment, score, protocol or GitHub state, and does not rescore the task. The grouped quality run is still active at this review's cutoff; partial output is not its result.

**The raw candidate has a real, bounded answer-quality regression under the authored criterion. A higher aggregate score does not pass that criterion. The pinned stack does not supply evidence for a one-switch batch-invariant repair. Finish the existing finite comparison, retain this limitation, and distinguish the incremental grouped-P change from the already present raw/native difference.** This does not require proving universal native equivalence or abandoning every experimental performance benefit.

## Result that must remain unchanged

The terminal [raw review](../quality/raw-quality-review-v1/README.md) and [case-level receipt](../quality/raw-quality-review-v1/review.json) report all 200 cases from each arm. Each native control scores 165; raw scores 166. The stable intersection contains 165 native-correct cases, and raw loses three of them while gaining four other answers. The strict gate and the supplementary answer gate both fail. Supplementary collection and actual mixed transaction coverage pass, with 25/25 batches and 250 transactions. A successful scorer process is not a passing score.

| Stable-set loss | Native1 = Native2 | Raw | First differing generated token, one-based |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Chemistry 03766, batch 3 peer | B, natural stop, 1,018 tokens | A, natural stop, 6,396 tokens | 66; common prefix 65 |
| Computer science 10672, batch 6 peer | A, natural stop, 1,198 tokens | G, natural stop, 1,046 tokens | 17; common prefix 16 |
| Law 01741, batch 14 peer | E, natural stop, 377 tokens | Length at 8,192, scored wrong | 14; common prefix 13 |

The native controls have identical saved arrays for these cases. Consequently the two natural-stop errors cannot be explained away as the earlier strict protocol's truncation problem, and historical native variation is not evidence that these particular losses are control noise. The new law truncation is also a candidate loss under the unchanged budget. Three of the four gains rescue native truncations; the fourth changes a naturally stopped wrong answer. Every loss and gain remains in the original report.

These observations establish failure of the bounded nonregression condition. They do not establish a population accuracy decrease, identify KV corruption, prove a floating-point mechanism, or show that every prompt is affected. The paired interval includes changes of either sign; neither that interval nor a nonsignificant test establishes equivalence. The user did not demand the internally chosen zero-new-error rule as a universal product requirement, but that fact cannot turn this experiment's failed rule into a pass.

Input identities retained by this review:

- Strict result: `3bfbaa4a225eb68fc0255ca0b24fc34acd099bd0d982ee7a8c12d59a56a9a393`.
- Supplementary result: `3ace6278fa0899e8bf8db0f3a5b6dcea873622cf178aee1117f5dbf2cfe84c86`.
- Frozen v2 plan file: `4d00042b237825e6c6d246013276fcf8025be55e6d8f7a16841d4069f9898e5b`.
- Frozen cases: `e00beeb8a4a548f184054d4feb45e3ca92cfa541b5ba59643380929c4faeac53`.

These are the existing receipt's identities, not a claim that weights or archived runs were newly revalidated here.

## What the pinned source permits us to say

The detailed independent [operator audit](../groupfx/batch-invariant-audit-v1/SOURCE-AUDIT.md), SHA256 `6a88e16ac541b48dae95bff11913a554c478721c1b2ee52d7cd8bc9fdad2888d`, binds its source and non-importing package discovery separately. Its pins are Core `d0f22d2bda562156e4dbf433ce645e1769b4f804`, Ascend `03766ac696fde5ab1980d80ca0b8543d3580c989`, ordinary plugin f7 `f7ed21e183b27d2141176f3e7a5c8a5ff4ede07d`, and source98 `98eac12dc2595a8742a81b58e96b899fed905194`. Ascend's previously recorded `csrc/build_aclnn.sh` modification is not a new audit change.

| Question | Concrete source finding | Consequence for this failure |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Is `VLLM_BATCH_INVARIANT=1` just stronger HCCL? | Ascend `batch_invariant.py:76–161` sets weight NZ mode 0, HCCL `strict`, LCCL `1`, torch deterministic algorithms with `warn_only=True`, and replaces selected operators. `worker/worker.py:969–981` invokes it before distributed initialization. | It is a bundled new configuration affecting P and D. Warn-only is not a complete unsupported-kernel guard. Any new outcome could not be attributed solely to HCCL. |
| Are the full invariant operators installed? | Non-importing discovery finds Triton, but no `batch_invariant_ops` or `flash_attn_npu_v3`; it deliberately does not initialize the Triton driver. | Even the narrower Triton branch remains runtime-unqualified. A flag or build invocation does not establish installed operator coverage. |
| What would the available branch replace? | `batch_invariant.py:107–136` registers selected aten matrix/softmax operations. FIA, add-RMSNorm and sum patches are in the optional AscendC branch, absent from the Triton-only branch. Its own `:50–61` explains why residual add-RMSNorm needs replacement. | Relevant math remains outside this switch's explicit coverage. Dense linear coverage cannot be generalized to the full hybrid model. |
| Are GDN and MoE covered? | Ascend Qwen GDN selects its backend directly, bypassing Core's generic Mamba capability check. P/D use custom convolution/state kernels and separate Triton kernels. BF16 MoE uses `npu_grouped_matmul`, plus routing operations, outside aten mm replacement. | There is no demonstrated shape-invariant whole-Qwen3.5 path here. A custom library may already be imported by camem, so the audit does **not** assert inevitable missing symbols or startup failure. Continued execution would still not prove invariant kernels. |
| Is the graph recipe supported unchanged? | Ascend's pinned `docs/source/user_guide/feature_guide/batch_invariance.md:111–142` lists Qwen3 models, not Qwen3.5 hybrid GDN, and documents no invariant-attention support for `FULL`/`FULL_DECODE_ONLY`. Plugin `src/vllm_muxpd/decode_graph.py:57–62` requires non-eager `FULL_DECODE_ONLY`. | There is no documented supported intersection with this fixed decode-graph recipe. This is a support/qualification gap, not proof that every default-FIA call must crash. Switching to eager or another graph mode would change the experiment and violate the present plugin graph contract. |
| Does source98 accept `strict` as the same profile? | `src/vllm_muxpd/determinism.py:26–39` requires literal `true` and batch invariance absent/0 when its optional policy is enabled; `:43–68` rejects an existing `strict` value. Worker validates before native initialization and reads back after group creation. | This intentionally prevents silently relabeling a different treatment. The ordinary f7 quality failure was not caused by this later guard. Do not loosen the guard and reuse the old profile identity. |

The Ascend build script has a separate 910B/A3 custom-op installation path that downloads versioned external installers/extensions. Its success messages alone do not prove successful imports or kernel support. Installing those dependencies now would alter the shared environment during an active experiment and still leave the GDN/MoE/graph questions; no installation was performed or is recommended as an immediate repair.

The official HCCL documentation distinguishes `true` reduction determinism from `strict` reduction-order control and describes platform/topology restrictions and possible performance cost. The independently cited documentation is for CANN 9.0, while this campaign uses 9.1. Source and documentation therefore support investigating a separate communication factor only after checking actual applicability; they do not establish a successful single-server repair. Neither setting makes local GDN, MoE, normalization and attention operations shape invariant. [HCCL setting documentation](https://www.hiascend.com/doc_center/source/zh/canncommercial/900/API/hcclug/hcclenvref_07_0010.html).

No new cost number follows from this source audit. Different GEMM kernels, disabling guarded custom ops/NZ, splitting fused operations, or changing graph support can all change runtime cost. Their measured cost and task quality would belong to a separately named configuration. Existing HCCL=true performance results do not measure a strict or batch-invariant configuration, and matching repeated outputs under true does not imply general batch invariance.

## Smallest useful diagnostic, without another acceptance experiment

The saved-token comparison is already the useful first step; do not repeat it as new evidence or launch another complete 200-case search with a different HCCL value. A single bounded localization probe is reasonable **if the owner wants to investigate mechanism**, but it is not a new mandatory universal gate or a way to repair the recorded score.

Start with **computer-science 10672, original batch 6**. It is a naturally stopped wrong answer with an early divergence, so this probe need not reach a final answer or the output cap. Retain the batch's eight original cases in order: 10611 (seed), 10672, 10734, 06847, 06907, 06967, 07028, 07090; their frozen prompts total 11,159 tokens. Keep the original sampling/budget, seed-first admission after the seed's first token, model/backends, HCCL=true, TP4/EP4 and each role's original native-chunked/plugin-full-P behavior. Do not isolate the target request and call that the same batch comparison.

Use a separate diagnostic source/attempt with a finite stop and cleanup after capturing the target through its first divergent position. Preserve the 8,192 request budget even when the diagnostic supervisor stops early; changing output capacity can change admission/allocation behavior. Do not continue to 8,192 merely to reconstruct an already known score. Native and raw each need one bounded capture; a repeated control is only warranted if capture does not reproduce the saved prefix or otherwise raises an observed ambiguity.

Record the target's exact prompt and generated-prefix identity, actual request-to-row/slot mapping, current P/D token shapes, co-batch prefixes, GDN/KV ownership and relevant execution counters. Capture a narrow rank-zero logits window around generated positions 16–18, with all-rank selected-token receipts and the logits for both observed candidate tokens. Report top-two margins, including ties; a small margin is evidence of sensitivity, not proof of harmlessness. Preserve raw values or full rows for these few positions so a top-k display does not hide a different winner. Diagnostics must use the established ordinary-worker extension route rather than a worker subclass rejected by the production scheduler.

Before comparing logit values, verify the target has the same prefix as the retained run and bind the actual contexts. Native and raw intentionally have different scheduling/batch contexts; comparing their logits can locate a divergence, but cannot be labeled a same-context numerical-equivalence test merely because the target prefix matches. Other requests may already differ. Replaying batch 6 alone also changes preceding-batch/cache history relative to the original 200-case process. If the divergence or prefix is not reproduced, report that limitation and stop the proposed probe; do not repeatedly change shapes, warmup or controls until a favorable result appears.

If this bounded capture reproduces the divergence and justifies further localization, inspect a narrow layer boundary around GDN state update or MoE routing/expert output. A larger state/hook probe should follow a concrete early mismatch, not be required for every tensor. A teacher-forced common-prefix comparison, if later needed, is a separately identified diagnostic because it changes generated histories. None of these probes changes the frozen answer result or proves physical overlap/performance.

Do **not** begin with blind full-model batch invariance or a full-200 strict rerun. The audited coverage and graph limitations make that a larger, different engineering project with uncertain benefit. A strict-only test could be a separate finite factor after actual platform applicability is established, with batch invariance off and all other settings matched; even success would show intervention sensitivity, not a unique root cause or automatic passage of the original quality gate.

## Finite disposition and remaining work

This applies the existing [closure review](CLOSURE-GATE-INDEPENDENT-REVIEW.md) to the new failed candidate result. That review's conditional use of a qualified supplement has **not** been satisfied by raw. It did not authorize changing the criterion after seeing candidate answers. The user authorizes autonomous development and merges and asks for a useful scene-specific benefit; the user did not request arbitrary-text equality, every tensor bit equal, or a universally best native comparison.

| Item | Honest current disposition | Finite remaining scope |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Raw vs native quality / #23 | Recorded task-quality nonregression failure, with complete collection and observed mixed work. Keep the known regression visible and the issue unresolved as a quality problem. | Finish the already running group arm and apply the unchanged scorers. Publish all four arms, transitions, failures and source/config boundaries. The suggested first-divergence probe is optional fault localization, not another acceptance rule. |
| Group vs raw increment | A different question from native equivalence. Existing short numerical and timing-array comparisons are bounded evidence; the active full task-quality arm is not yet a result. | Compare all 200 group/raw answers, finish statuses and saved token arrays once terminal. If they match, report preservation of the measured raw behavior, including the three native-relative losses. If they differ, retain every additional transition; do not let raw's existing failure mask an added regression. |
| #11 | Its completed, source/config/input-bounded numerical investigation remains valid. | Do not reopen its historical artifact identities or call its closure a general-quality certificate. Link the newly demonstrated task-quality limitation to #23. |
| #3 and value | The measured group/raw throughput increment, worst-gap tradeoff and real lifecycle evidence remain valid within their stated recipes. They do not certify quality or an HCCL-strict configuration. | Finish the already prepared practical native reference and actual mixed-work profile, retaining failures and negative results. Keep the native/plugin mixed-token difference as a limit on pure scheduling-cost attribution. No new universal kernel-overlap or resource-partition requirement is introduced. |
| PR #10 | Keep draft while the existing finite assessment is incomplete. This raw failure predates the incremental grouping comparison and cannot simply be assigned to grouped compilation. | After the complete group comparison and existing performance/profile work, make an explicit experimental merge or measured rejection decision. An opt-in merge may preserve a documented inherited limitation, but it cannot be described as passing native-relative quality. If no acceptable supported quality boundary or mitigation is available, retain the known quality issue or reject the candidate rather than declare it fixed. |

An experimental merge and an issue's “fixed” resolution are different acts. If group preserves raw on the completed fixed set and the bounded operational/performance evidence supports the increment, merging that optional implementation can be a reasoned product decision with the failed native-relative quality outcome prominent. It must not imply a generally safe quality substitution, default activation, or source98/wheel task-quality qualification beyond the actual bridge. Conversely, there is no need to demand endless arbitrary-input proof before making an honest rejection or maintaining an experimental feature with a known open limitation.

Do not raise the cap again, drop the three losses, switch parser, retroactively replace the stable-case veto with aggregate accuracy, select favorable retries, or redefine a failed gate to obtain closure. Do not infer that a matching group arm repairs raw. If no concrete mechanism is isolated by a bounded probe, the truthful end state is a documented unresolved quality limitation alongside the measured scene-specific value, not a fabricated root cause.

The issue/PR scope references are [#23](https://github.com/vLLM-HUST/vllm-ascend-MuxPD-hust/issues/23), [#3](https://github.com/vLLM-HUST/vllm-ascend-MuxPD-hust/issues/3), and [draft #10](https://github.com/vLLM-HUST/vllm-ascend-MuxPD-hust/pull/10). Published performance/lifecycle scope is the immutable [v4 report](https://github.com/vLLM-HUST/vllm-hust-benchmark/tree/ef849ef49577c491fee68317ffc96d52796cd379/reports/muxpd-followup-20261025/performance-lifecycle-v4). Raw quality is newer local terminal evidence at this cutoff, excluded from that fixed v4 publication; this review does not claim it is already in those assets.

A concrete, unposted [Issue #23 update and finite task list](ISSUE23-RAW-FAILURE-FOLLOWUP-DRAFT-v1.md) accompanies this review. It leaves the substantive quality limitation open and separates an eventual opt-in incremental merge decision from a false “quality fixed” disposition.

Coordinator disposition after reading this review: retain #23 as an unresolved known quality limitation; finish the existing group-quality, practical native-default-v2 and mixed-profiler work; do not start the optional localization probe in this campaign absent a concrete repair lead. An eventual opt-in PR #10 merge can be considered if group preserves raw and the finite assessment supports the measured increment. #3 can receive a completed measured-optimization disposition with tradeoffs and a link to the still-open quality limitation. This records a work-scope decision, not a changed experimental acceptance rule.
